Let $AD$ trysect $BC$ into three equal parts. Let those three points be $D_0,D_1,D_2.$ Let the segment $D_0D_1$ have a length of $ 10 \sqrt3$ (because $BC$ has a length of $20$ and the medial segment $D_1D_2$ has a length of 10), then $AD$ has a length of $40 \sqrt3.$ Since $BD_1=BD_0+D_0D_1=10 \sqrt3+3 \sqrt6,$ and $D_1$ is the midpoint of $BC$ then $BD_2=$ $BD_0+3 \sqrt6+D_0D_1=10 \sqrt3+9 \sqrt6.$ Similarly $CD_2= 10 \sqrt3+9 \sqrt6.$
Now since $40 \sqrt3=AD=BD_2+CD_2,CD_2+BD_2+BC-CD_2-BC)=120 \sqrt3.$
Now as $ 120 \sqrt3=BC-CD_1- BD_1,BC-10 \sqrt3-10 \sqrt3=120 \sqrt3.$ From here we see that $BC=150 \sqrt3.$

Now, we have this formula $[ABC]=r*(s-h),$ $r=AD/2=20 \sqrt3,$ $h=CD_1+BD_1+AD=198 \sqrt3-75 \sqrt3=\mathrm{160 \sqrt3},$ and $s=\frac{BC+AD+CD}{2}=210 \sqrt3$ so $\mathrm{[ABC]}~r*(s-h)=$ $20 \sqrt3 (210 \sqrt3 -161 \sqrt3)=2520.$

I was unsure what to do after that, so I decided to look at the answer in the back of the current released SAT. I can guarantee a student scored better than I in the current released SAT during the last two weeks, so I am confident I would not have done anything wrong here. Armed with the answer, I proceeded to do a quick length check using the area formula for a triangle so $[ABC]\ \cdot\ 2=\mathrm{7260},$ and I can figure out the values of $\mathrm{h},$ $r,$ and $s$ now. Then $8 \mathrm{h} = 1620,$ $8s=4140,$ and $3 \mathrm{r} = 1620.$ This gives me that $\mathrm{s-h}=\frac{s+r-h}{2}=\frac{150}{2}=75$, which matches. So $\mathrm{[ABC]}=2520$.


Now, we move on to the problem at hand. So, instead of contacting what $\mathrm{s}$ is, we notice that we can define $\mathrm{h'}=\frac{[ABC]}{4}=630$ and $\mathrm{s'}=\frac{s}{2}=2070,$ and we hope they are both integers. From this, we can get $\frac{\mathrm{s}^{2}}{\mathrm{s'}}=\frac{[ABC]^{2}}{2520^{2}}=\frac{11925}{6706667}=\frac{n}{m}.$ So $\mathrm{m+n}=11925+6706667=\boxed{6718592}.$

Here's a quick physical description of the solution.

[A comment: I am not sure whether I would recommend trying to draw a scaled ratio picture here given that time constraint, but there could be an attempt to go around it with paper/pencil. For my method I treated the $ABC$ triangle as a plateau balanced in the middle by the incircle, and I drew pending figures with similarly sized sides until I could simplify the setup into a two-variable expression.] Let $r$ denote the inradius and $\Delta$ the area. Then $r=\frac{\Delta}{2s}$ where $s$ is the semiperimeter. From the question we get that for some $m$ and $n,$ $\Delta=m\sqrt{n}.$ Then $s=\frac{20+m\sqrt{n}}{2}.$

Then $AD=s+2r=\frac{20+m\sqrt{n}}{2}+m\sqrt{n}.$ Also, let $BD=\frac{m\sqrt{n}+22\sqrt{3}}{2}+a$ and $CD=\frac{22\sqrt{3}-22\sqrt{3}a+m\sqrt{n}}{2}$ for some $-a<-1.\,$ Note that $\Delta=\frac{(m\sqrt{n}-22\sqrt{3})^2a}{4}-2r$ but upon substituting $B, C, D$ with their respective coordinates and expanding $[ABC]$ gives $\Delta=m\sqrt{n}-208\sqrt{3}.$

Then $[ABC]=\frac{\left\{\left(\frac{20+m\sqrt{n}}{2}\right)^2+b_c+b_d\right\}\left(\frac{22\sqrt{3}-22\sqrt{3}a+m\sqrt{n}}{2}\right)+\left(\frac{20+m\sqrt{n}}{2}\right)^2+b_c+b_d\right($, where $b_c$ and $b_d$ are the $C$ and $D$ feet from $A, B$ respectively. Completing the squares in the sets we get that $\Delta=\frac{185\sqrt{3}+m^2}{4}-2r=\frac{5\sqrt{3}-2a}{4},$ so $\Delta/2=-\frac{5\sqrt{3}+2a}{8}=m\sqrt{n}-208\sqrt{3}.$ Therefore $a=\frac{8m\sqrt{n}+1664\sqrt{3}}{5}$.

Had $\mathrm{BD},$ $\mathrm{BD'},$ and $\mathrm{CD}$ all have integral lengths, then so would $\mathrm{a},$ and one says that $m=2$ and $n=\boxed{369}.$ I checked the assumption that $a$ must have integral length. $\mathrm{a} = \frac{8m\sqrt{369}+1664\sqrt{3}}{5}$ is indeed a linear rise, so negating it doesn't break the relationship it satisfied. Thus, the solution is correct.

[asy]
unitsize(2cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(12pt)+linewidth(.95));
pair F=(1,0), G=(2,0), H=(3,0), B=(20,0);
pair Dm=B+E/2, D2=B+G/2, D=G+B/3, A=-Dm+F, E=(E+F)/2, F2=2E-F, G2=(G+F)/2, H2=(H+F)/2;
draw(F--G, dashed);
real isin=((G-F)*(G-H))/((G-E)*(G-F));
fill((G-E)*isin--(G+F)*(1-isin)--B--cycle,gray);
fill(Dm--D2--EF*isin*1/3-Dm--cycle,lightgray);
fill(D2--DH*isin--G--cycle,lightgray);
draw(F--B,Dthin);
draw(H2--Dm, red+thick);
draw(Dm--G2, red+thick);
draw(Dm--G2+sqrt(3)*dir(-15)*X);
MP("A",Dm,NNW);
MP("H",G2,D--W);
MP("B",B,S);
dot("|EB--EB|");
dot(F);
dot(B);
dot(D=H2+F/2,linewidth(.5));
[/asy]
